WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.040
Vast stretches of Central Asia feel eerily uninhabited.
中亚的大片地区给人一种荒无人烟的诡异之感。

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:15.380
Fly at 30,000 feet over the southern part of the former Soviet Union and there are long moments when no town or road or field is visible from your window.
在30,000英尺的高空飞越前苏联南部，从舷窗望出去，时常会有很长一段时间看不到任何城镇、道路或农田。

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:22.050
The landscape of stark desert, trackless steppe, and rugged mountains seems to swallow up anything human.
这里的地貌由荒凉的沙漠、无路可循的草原和崎岖的群山构成，似乎会吞没一切与人类有关的东西。

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.610
It is little surprise, then, that this region remains largely unknown to most archaeologists.
因此，大多数考古学家对这一地区至今仍知之甚少，也就不足为奇了。

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:40.780
Wandering bands and tribes roamed this immense area for 5,000 years, herding goats, sheep, cattle, and horses across immense steppes, through narrow valleys, and over high snowy passes.
在长达5,000年的时间里，四处漂泊的游群和部落在这片广袤的土地上游荡，赶着山羊、绵羊、牛和马，穿越辽阔的草原，穿过狭窄的山谷，翻过白雪覆盖的高山隘口。

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:48.660
They left occasional tombs that survived the ages, and on rare occasions settled down and built towns or even cities.
他们零星留下了一些历经漫长岁月保存至今的墓葬，在极少数情况下还会定居下来，建造城镇乃至城市。

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:56.450
But for the most part, these peoples left behind few physical traces of their origins, beliefs, or ways of life.
但总体而言，这些民族留下的有关自身起源、信仰或生活方式的实物痕迹寥寥无几。

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:10.130
What we know of these nomadic pastoralists comes mainly from their periodic forays into India, the Middle East, and China, where they often wreaked havoc and earned a fearsome reputation as enemies of urban life.
我们对这些游牧民的了解，主要来自他们不时对印度、中东和中国发动的侵袭；他们在那些地方常常大肆破坏，落下了城市生活之敌的可怕名声。

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:28.150
In the past century, scholars criticized these people as destructive, dismissed them as marginal, or, at best, cast them as a harsh tonic for restoring vigor to decaying and soft agricultural societies from ancient Mesopotamia to Imperial Rome to Han China.
在过去的一个世纪里，学者们批评这些人具有破坏性，把他们贬为无足轻重的边缘群体，或者往好里说，把他们看作一剂猛烈的补药，能让从古代美索不达米亚到罗马帝国再到汉代中国的那些日渐衰败、柔弱萎靡的农业社会重振活力。

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:45.450
In the 1950s, the British archaeologist Mortimer Wheeler blamed the aggressive, chariot-driving Aryans who swept in from the steppes for the demise of the peaceful Indus River civilization after 1800 B.C., though later archaeologists dismissed that claim.
20世纪50年代，英国考古学家莫蒂默·惠勒把爱好和平的印度河文明在公元前1800年后的消亡，归咎于从草原席卷而来、驾着战车的好战雅利安人，不过后来的考古学家否定了这一说法。

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:59.190
But Michael Frachetti, a young archaeologist at Washington University in St. Louis, takes the radical view that Central Asians were early midwives in the birth of civilization rather than a destructive force.
但圣路易斯华盛顿大学的年轻考古学家迈克尔·弗拉切蒂持有一种激进的观点：中亚人是文明诞生之初的助产士，而不是一股破坏性力量。

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:17.020
Frachetti argues that ancient pastoralists living in the third millennium B.C., at the time of the first great cities of Mesopotamia, Egypt, and the Indus, created a network stretching across thousands of miles that passed along goods, technologies, and ideas central to urban life.
弗拉切蒂认为，公元前三千纪正值美索不达米亚、埃及和印度河流域第一批大城市出现之时，生活在那个时期的古代牧民建立了一个绵延数千英里的网络，传递着对城市生活至关重要的商品、技术和思想。

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.540
He believes they helped create civilization rather than hindering it.
他认为，这些牧民帮助创造了文明，而不是阻碍了文明。

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:31.390
Most archaeological work in Central Asia during the past century has focused on the open and rolling plains that stretch from the Black Sea to Manchuria.
过去一个世纪里，中亚的考古工作大多集中在从黑海一直延伸到中国东北的那片开阔起伏的平原上。

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:42.880
These steppes only came to life after 2000 B.C., when horse domestication and riding suddenly turned a forbidding landscape for pedestrians into a natural highway of grass.
这些草原直到公元前2000年以后才焕发生机：那时，人们驯化了马，又学会了骑马，这片令徒步者望而生畏的土地便一下子变成了一条天然的青草大道。

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.810
By contrast, the areas to the south of the steppes have long been dismissed as backwaters of history.
相比之下，草原以南的地区长期以来一直被贬为远离历史主流的闭塞之地。

00:02:50.610 --> 00:03:01.190
In the past, these southern mountains and deserts were considered too remote, rugged, and inhospitable to have played a role in early migrations or the emergence of urban life.
过去，人们认为南部这些山脉和沙漠太过偏远、崎岖，又不适宜居住，不可能在早期的迁徙或城市生活的兴起中发挥过作用。

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:17.530
The Karakum Desert, where it might rain once in a decade, covers nearly two-thirds of today’s Turkmenistan, while the perpetually snow-covered Tian Shan Mountains of western China and eastern Kyrgyzstan soar 24,000 feet into the thin air.
卡拉库姆沙漠可能十年才下一场雨，覆盖了今天土库曼斯坦近三分之二的国土；而中国西部和吉尔吉斯斯坦东部终年积雪的天山山脉则高达24,000英尺，直插空气稀薄的高空。

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:31.900
It is there that Frachetti and a new generation of archaeologists from the United States and Central Asian nations are discovering evidence of a network of pastoralists who thrived centuries before hooves resounded on the steppes to the north.
正是在那里，弗拉切蒂和来自美国及中亚各国的新一代考古学家不断发现证据，表明曾存在一个由牧民组成的网络；早在北面草原上响起马蹄声的几个世纪之前，这些牧民就已兴旺发达。

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:44.040
These forgotten peoples may have carried such markers of civilization as ceramics and grains across thousands of miles, two millennia before the Silk Road linked the Roman Empire with Han China.
这些被遗忘的民族可能曾带着陶器和谷物这类文明的标志跨越数千英里，比丝绸之路连通罗马帝国与汉代中国还要早两千年。

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:58.890
Frachetti argues that the new data emerging from the region force archaeologists to rethink their ideas about trade across Eurasia during the Bronze Age, when the first civilizations were taking form to the east, south, and west.
弗拉切蒂认为，这一地区不断涌现的新数据迫使考古学家重新审视自己对青铜时代欧亚大陆贸易往来的看法；那时，最早的一批文明正在东面、南面和西面逐渐成形。

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:13.580
Frachetti, who has studied modern-day pastoralists in such unforgiving landscapes as the Sahara and Scandinavia, was drawn to the southern region of Central Asia for its environmental diversity of desert, grassland, and meadows.
弗拉切蒂曾在撒哈拉和斯堪的纳维亚等条件严酷的地带研究过现代牧民，他之所以被中亚南部吸引，是因为那里环境多样，既有沙漠，也有草地和草甸。

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:22.120
Instead of a wasteland, he saw an ideal landscape for enterprising herders who wanted to pasture their animals in all seasons.
他看到的不是一片荒原，而是一处理想之地，适合那些富有进取心、希望一年四季都能放牧牲畜的牧人。

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.380
Together with his colleagues, Frachetti began digging a decade ago in the Dzhungar Mountains of Kazakhstan.
十年前，弗拉切蒂与同事们一起开始在哈萨克斯坦的准噶尔山脉发掘。

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:41.600
Covering nearly 500 square miles, this region lies between the Tian Shan and Altai mountain ranges, and boasts sharp peaks topping 12,000 feet, as well as harsh desert.
这一地区面积近500平方英里，位于天山山脉和阿尔泰山脉之间，既有高度超过12,000英尺的险峻山峰，也有环境恶劣的沙漠。

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:59.840
At a site near a village called Begash, on a flat terrace enclosed by steep canyon walls alongside a small stream, the team uncovered the foundations of simple stone structures along with an array of potsherds and bronze and stone artifacts in stone-lined oval and rectangular tombs.
在一个名叫别加什的村庄附近，有一处遗址坐落在小溪旁的一块平坦台地上，四周是陡峭的峡谷崖壁；考古队在这里发掘出了简易石头建筑的地基，还在用石块砌边的椭圆形和长方形墓葬中发现了一批陶片以及青铜器和石器。

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:11.230
The earliest layers at Begash date to at least as early as 2500 B.C., based on accelerator mass spectrometry dating of organic remains, says Frachetti.
弗拉切蒂说，根据对有机遗存的加速器质谱测年，别加什最早的地层至少可以追溯到公元前2500年。

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:20.620
One woman was laid to rest with a bell-shaped hooked bronze earring around 1700 B.C., according to electron spin resonance dating.
根据电子自旋共振测年，大约在公元前1700年，一名女子带着一只钟形带钩的青铜耳环下葬。

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:30.950
Similar earrings are only found several centuries later some 600 miles to the north on the Siberian steppes, hinting at styles that moved north over time.
类似的耳环直到几个世纪之后，才见于北面约600英里外的西伯利亚草原，暗示这些样式随着时间推移向北传播。

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:43.350
More surprisingly, the excavators found wheat, which was first domesticated in the Fertile Crescent of the Middle East, and broomcorn millet that was first widely grown in northern China.
更令人意外的是，发掘者发现了小麦和黍：小麦最早是在中东的新月沃地驯化的，而黍最早是在中国北方广泛种植的。

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:56.130
The grains were used ritually in a burial, and radiocarbon dating of the remains dates them to about 2200 B.C., making them the oldest known domesticated grains in Central Asia.
这些谷物曾在一处墓葬中用于仪式；对遗存的放射性碳测年表明，它们的年代约为公元前2200年，因而成为中亚已知最古老的驯化谷物。

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:08.330
The people of Begash may not have grown either grain—there are no grinding stones, a sign of grain preparation—but instead received it via trade networks stretching from the Near East to China.
别加什人可能两种谷物都没种过——那里没有磨石，而磨石是加工谷物的标志——而是通过从近东一直延伸到中国的贸易网络得到了谷物。

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:17.350
Dorian Fuller, a leading expert in ancient grains based at University College London, calls the finds “important and well dated.”
伦敦大学学院的多里安·富勒是研究古代谷物的权威专家，他称这些发现“意义重大，年代测定可靠”。

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:33.730
He adds that Chinese crops such as millet began to appear in southwest Asia around 1900 B.C., a few centuries after they reached Begash, which could mean the passage through the mountain regions was a means of gradual transmission from east to west.
他还说，黍等中国作物在公元前1900年左右开始出现在西南亚，比它们到达别加什晚了几个世纪，这可能意味着，穿越这些山区的通道是由东向西逐步传播的一种途径。

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:44.490
Frachetti speculates that the grains may have been acquired from other tribes and used for ritual purposes, and then perhaps were passed on in turn to other pastoral peoples.
弗拉切蒂推测，这些谷物可能是从其他部落那里得来、用于仪式的，之后或许又辗转传给了其他畜牧民族。

00:06:45.290 --> 00:07:08.530
What makes the Begash discoveries so important is that previously this region was assumed to have been a land of scattered foragers until steppe peoples trickled down into the area’s valleys and mountain ranges after 2000 B.C. But it is becoming evident that the people of Begash were not simple foragers, but sophisticated pastoralists who tended their flocks, much as people in the area still do today.
别加什的发现之所以如此重要，是因为过去人们认为，在公元前2000年以后草原民族陆续南下、进入该地区的山谷和山脉之前，这里是零散的狩猎采集者生活的地方。但越来越明显的是，别加什人并非简单的狩猎采集者，而是成熟老练的牧民，他们照料着自己的畜群，就像如今这一带的人依然在做的那样。

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:25.450
The inhabitants did not begin to use horses until well into the second millennium B.C., and the varieties of sheep and goat found here today appear to be related to the varieties first domesticated thousands of years before in western Iran, near ancient Mesopotamia.
直到进入公元前二千纪很久以后，这里的居民才开始使用马匹；而如今在这里见到的绵羊和山羊品种，似乎与此前数千年在古代美索不达米亚附近的伊朗西部最早驯化的品种有亲缘关系。

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:35.570
This indicates that Begash was “at the crossroads of extremely wide networks among Eurasian communities by the third millennium B.C.,” asserts Frachetti.
弗拉切蒂断言，这表明别加什“到公元前三千纪时，已处于欧亚大陆各族群间极为广泛的网络交汇之处”。

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:40.800
That doesn’t mean that traders traversed thousands of miles in this early period.
这并不意味着早在这一时期，商人就已跋涉数千英里。

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:50.980
Instead, the archaeologist envisions pastoralists taking their flocks to higher pastures in the summer, where they encountered neighbors from other valleys doing the same.
这位考古学家设想的情形则是：牧民夏天把畜群赶到地势更高的牧场，并在那里遇到来自其他山谷、同样赶着畜群上山的邻人。

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:58.220
Thus, ideas and technologies might have passed gradually through the mountain corridor of southern Central Asia.
因此，思想和技术可能是经由中亚南部的山地走廊逐渐传播的。

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:07.250
This corridor, Frachetti believes, may have been a key conduit for Bronze Age developments farther into East Asia and Mongolia.
弗拉切蒂认为，这条走廊可能曾是青铜时代各项发展成果进一步传入东亚和蒙古的关键通道。
